{"name":"Polar Bear","hex":"#eae9e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"polar bear","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A white, semi-aquatic, hypercarnivorous species of bear, Ursus maritimus, whose native range lies largely within the Arctic Circle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/polar_bear"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Polar Bear","blocks":[{"text":"The Polar Bear is a 1998 German action film directed by Til Schweiger."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/2d/The_Polar_Bear_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Polar_Bear","wikidata":"Q1193109"},{"title":"Polar Bears (The Naked Brothers Band)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Polar Bears\" is the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth episodes of the second season of the television series The Naked Brothers Band, which premiered as a television movie special on Nickelodeon on June 6, 2008 to 1.7 million viewers for children aged 6–11; it was the second most watched show for the week in that age group."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_Bears_(The_Naked_Brothers_Band)","wikidata":"Q7209024"},{"title":"Polar bear","blocks":[{"text":"The polar bear is a large bear native to the Arctic and nearby areas. It is closely related to the brown bear, and the two species can interbreed. The polar bear is the largest extant species of bear and land carnivore by body mass, with adult males weighing 300–800 kg (660–1,760 lb). The species is sexually dimorphic, as adult females are much smaller. The polar bear is white or yellowish-furred with black skin and a thick layer of fat. It is more slender than the brown bear, with a narrower skull, longer neck and lower shoulder hump. Its teeth are sharper and more adapted to cutting meat. The paws are large and allow the bear to walk on ice and paddle in the water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_bear","wikidata":"Q33609"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear","wikidata":"Q11788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Polarbrown-1.jpg/330px-Polarbrown-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Polarbrown-1.jpg/330px-Polarbrown-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Polar/brown bear hybrid taxidermy specimen on display at Natural History Museum at Tring in Hertfordshire, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolarbrown-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Ursus_maritimus_02_MWNH_420.JPG/500px-Ursus_maritimus_02_MWNH_420.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Ursus_maritimus_02_MWNH_420.JPG/1280px-Ursus_maritim